The function `moveOneBall` rearranges a list of integers such that all non-zero values are moved to even indices. It returns `true` if at least one element was successfully placed in an even index after swapping, and `false` otherwise.

Here's how it works:

- The algorithm uses two pointers (`left` starting from the beginning and `right` from the end).
- It finds the first non-zero value on the left and the first zero on the right.
- If found, they swap places, moving non-zero values to the left side of the array.
- This continues until no more swaps can be made or when there are no zeros remaining.
- After rearrangement, we check if any non-zero number is at an even index (even position), which means at least one ball has been moved as required.

